opean maze so this  results are suggesting that neurons in  the rodents can signal the spatial  boundaries and they are capable of  capturing the geometric structure of the  space  then the next question is what do we see  similar neural responses in humans  and whether those neurons also respond  to the cognitive boundaries in the  non-spatial domains  and further whether the boundary  responses kind of reflect the subsequent  memory formation or retrieval  so to address all these questions we  developed a spatial we developed memory  tasks which consists of three different  stages  during encoding we presented a series of  clips to the subjects with no sound  and the key manipulation here is that  we're inventing different types of  cognitive boundaries into the clips  for the no boundary clips there's no  manual added it's just a continuous  movie shot  and for the comparison reasons we use  the middle of the clip as our virtual  boundary for the analysis  and for the soft boundaries it contains  typical hollywood style cuts  so it re the thought boundary refers to  the transitions from related events in  the same movie  and for the hard boundary clips we  manually made it by putting two  different clips from two different  movies together  so the hard boundary actually refers to  the event transitions to the unrelated  events  so after subject watch all the clips  which is in 90 total clips  we evaluate subject's memory of those  clips they have watched in two different  ways  during series definition we extracted  frames from the clips they have seen  during encoding as target frames and  also frames they haven't seen from the  clips which is the foil frames  then we instructed subjects determining  whether the frames presented is old  which they have seen before or new which  they haven't seen before  we also evaluate subject's memory of the  temporal order of the clips  so we extract the frames from the videos  they have seen before and presented side  by side to the subject  and ask the subject to determine whether  the left or the right frame appears  first in original clips they have seen  besides the binary choice we provided  here we also invented some measurements  of subjects confidence levels  and in total this experiment has  performed on 20 subjects which are  patients undergoing surgical evaluations  with drug resistant epilepsy  so first i will go through some behavior  results when found  in the same recognition task for the  thin recognition accuracy if we plot it  against the relative distance between  the target frame and its reason past  preceding boundaries  what we found is that for the frames  right after soft boundary and heart  boundaries are tend to be better  remembered than the frames that were  further away from the soft and hard  boundaries  and such distant effects was not  presented in no boundary conditions  and for the time discrimination task  which extracted the frames across  different boundaries presented to the  subject side by site and asked them to  determine which one occurs the first  if we plotting subjects behavior results  in terms of their accuracy reaction time  and confidence ratings  what we found is that subjects tend to  have lower accuracy rate  and tend to take longer response time  and also are less sure about their  responses when trying to recall the  temporal order information across two  frames that were originally separated by  a hard boundary in the clips  so it seems like there are some  trade-off effects of the boundary  inference on episodic memory systems  we are looking into the neural data  trying to find any associated neural  correlates the technique we are using is  the neural links atlas system which  providing high sampling rates and we  were using the hybrid electrodes from  adtech  as shown here in the schematic plot  there are microwires spreading out on  the tip of each electroshank  which provide opportunities for us to  record in single-unit activity and also  the local field potential signals  so the whole implantation procedure is  solely driven by the clinical needs  the patients will take a  pre-implantation mri scan first  and the neurosurgeons and neurologists  will  will get the surgical planning based on  the clinical needs  and then they will inserted  macroelectrodes first and using the  electrode as a guidance to slide in the  microwires  and after the implantations  patients will get either a  post-implantation mri or ct  for help  better localize where the electrodes are  so on the top right you can see the  signals we recorded by using such  recording  particles now on the top row is the raw  signal you can already see some text on  top of the draw signals  which are  interactivity  and by applying the bypass filtering and  also using our open toolbox  o sort we were able to detect the spike  activities and group them together as  different clusters  across 20 subjects we recorded so far  we detect approximately a thousand  neurons across different brain regions  and for this study specifically we were  focusing on the medial temporal lobe  including the hippocampus medulla and  pure hippocampal drivers which have all  sodium supporting the episodic membrane  system in humans  as i mentioned before during the  encoding we presented different clips  embedded with different type of  boundaries to the subjects  so the first question is for the  recorded neurons we have how they  responded to this different type of  boundaries  so we plotted neurons responses against  different type of boundaries  and we found two different interesting  neurons  the first type we named as boundary  cells as shown in here is one boundary  cells we found  and each dot here represents the  activation of the neurons which is also  color coded for different boundary  conditions here  for boundary cells you can observe that  this neuron increase its fire rate after  soft and hard boundaries but doesn't  show any firing rate modulations before  or after the no boundary conditions  we also find another type of neurons we  name it as the event cells  and this is example event that we have  observed  so for event cells it only increases its  fine rate for the heart boundary  condition but not the other two boundary  types  of course across all the medial temporal  neurons we observed so far we have  detected 42 boundary cells and 36 event  cells  and they all show you very consistent  neuronal activity  for the boundary cells they all  responded to the soft and hard  boundaries and for the event cells they  only responded to the heart boundaries  one interesting question here is that  whether  the neural responses we observed to  associate with the boundaries is purely  driven by any visual transitions  since the no boundary doesn't have any  visual transition at all to address  these questions we also plotted the  neurons responses for all the boundary  cells and event cells when responding to  the clip onset  and also  as showing on the right side  these two group of neurons do not  respond to the clip onset clip offsets  the same way as they did for the  boundaries or for the soft and hard  boundaries  which suggests that  the observed boundary responses cannot  be simply explained by detecting of the  visual transitions but presumably  exporting or driven by high-level  covenant functions  another interesting information here is  that  since both boundary cells and vent cells  are responding to the hard boundaries  when we're plotting their responses  against the hard boundaries  we observed that the boundary cells  responds to the high boundary  approximately a hundred milliseconds  earlier than event cells  suggesting a hierarchical structure  might be preserved in the medial  temporal lobe and detecting the heart  boundary conditions  and interestingly the spatial  distribution of the boundary cells and  event cells are very localized  most of the boundary cells and event  cells are presented in the medial  temporal lobe including the medulla  hippocampus and pure hippocampal gyrus  regions  and on top of that most of the boundary  cells are located in the pure  hippocampal gyrus while the majority of  the event cells are in the hippocampus  so the next interesting question will be  whether the neural responses associated  with different boundaries also have some  inference or reflect certain subject  subsequent memory performance  to address these questions you will go  back to the original boundary cell i  showed you before  and this is the activation plot of that  boundary cell  if we further splitting into  splitting this  activation profiles  based on subject subsequent memory  performance in scene recognition whether  they get correct or incorrect  what we can see is that  the majority increased fine rate for the  soft and hard boundaries occurred in the  correct trials versus the incorrect  trials  and we further quantify such fire rate  modulations across all 42 boundary cells  consistently we see higher final rates  for the correct soft and hard boundary  trials compared to the incorrect ones  and we don't see such conditional  difference in the no boundary condition  then how about the event cells  for the event cells this is the same  cells i showed you before  such an activation plot we can also  convert it into a raster but into a  phase plot  by purely marking the spike in times of  each activation relative to the theta  phases  so then this activation plot will be  converted into a phase plot  with the x-axis still be the time zero  still be the time of the boundary and  y-axis becomes the phase  and we can further splitting this phase  plot based on subject subsequent memory  performance in the time discrimination  tasks  and then we observe that the spiking  timing of the uh  in the correct and  then we observe that the spiking timing  in the soft and hard correct trials tend  to clustered at specific phase  compared to the other four conditions  and we can further quantify this  clustering by using main resultant lens  computations  as shown in the screen line here  if the firing phase is uniformly  distributed we expect a  smaller main resultant length  as showing in the blue line here if they  all fire at consistent phase you're  expecting to have a larger mean  resultant length  across all 36 event cells we're seeing  larger  mean resultant lengths in the correct  soft and hard boundary trials compared  to the incorrect ones and we didn't  observe any conditional difference in  the no boundary condition  then besides the single  neuron activities we also look at at the  population level how the neural state  changes  across corresponding to the different  boundary conditions  to address this question  we constructed a neural dynamics matrix  by combining all the activity from all  india temporal cells we recorded across  the subjects during coding  and then we projected this high  dimensional neural activity into a low  dimensional space by applying the  principal component analysis methods  and on the right side you see the neural  state space constructing using the top  three pc component  and each dot in this plot representing a  neural state at the given time point  the black dot indicating when the  boundary occurs  and also the line thickness  increased across time  so for the no boundary conditions the  neural states entangle with each other  before and after the boundaries  however for the soft and hard boundaries  we do observe a large neural state shift  after the boundaries  we can further quantify  how this neural state looks like by  using the multi-dimensional  distance measurements the mdd  so on the right side consistent with the  plot on the left  we observe this neural state shifts in  both soft and hard boundaries but not in  the no boundary condition  and interestingly such magnitude of the  neural response shift in mdd also  correlated with our behavior data  if we quantify the magnitude using the  area under curve mdd within the one  second time window after boundaries  we observe that the a  area under curve mdd is positively  correlated with our scene recognition  accuracy but negatively correlated with  our time discrimination accuracy  this is also tied up with our previous  observed trade-off effects in the memory  performance  last but not least we also look at how  boundary shapes the memory retrieval  to address this question we computed the  correlation between the scene  recognition and encoding  for each subject we formed these specs  vectors  and computed the correlation between the  scene recognition and encoding at each  given time point  by applying a sliding window we ended up  will getting this correlation maps for  each  trials  with the warmer color denoting the  higher correlation coefficient and  colder color denoting the lower  correlation coefficient values  and we computed this for all the trials  and group them based on subject behavior  outcomes  whether the its correct target so they  correctly recognize the target as old  directly reject the foils as inc new  incorrect target they fastly recognize  the  target as new or incorrect foils they  fastly recognize the foil images as old  so for this subject we observed that a  higher correlation occurs after soft and  hard boundaries  for correct target and incorrect foils  such trend is very consistent across  subjects as well as we see in higher  correlation values after soft and hard  boundaries  in the correct target and incorrect foil  trials but not the other two conditions  so this is suggesting that the neural  state at the boundaries actually  reinstated during the memory retrieval  as well  so in summary  behaviorally we observe that the  boundaries enhance the recognition  accuracy  however the heart boundary actually  impairs the memory of the temporal order  at the single neuron levels we observed  78 neurons signaling different  boundaries in the medial temporal lobe  either they responded to the heart  boundary only or both soft and hard  boundaries  and interestingly the response strength  of these boundary cells in event cells  also correlates with subject's memory  performance  then at the population levels we observe  the neural state shift across boundaries  their magnitude is positively correlated  with same recognition accuracy but  negatively correlated with the time  discrimination accuracy  and last but not least the neural states  at the boundary reinstated during the  memory retrieval suggesting that the  boundary serves as the landmarks for the  mental time travel during memory  retrieval  before i closing the talk today i also  want to thank my pis and also our  brilliant colleagues from different  recording sites including toronto  western hospital cedars-sinai medical  center  boston children's hospitals  and our funding source the brain  initiative  and our lovely patient and also our  clinical teams  and i hope you enjoyed the talk and  thank you for your attention  you

